{"level":"error","message":"JSON STRING THAT FAILED TO PARSE: \n[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"8722a1a5-2199-4efa-90a4-e4fc8929b48e\",\n    \"ruleId\": \"DS-0026\",\n    \"attackVector\": \"An attacker exploits this through a 'Resource Exhaustion' attack. By submitting a repository for audit that triggers complex or infinite-loop test cases within the sandbox, the attacker causes the containerized processes to hang. Since there is no HEALTHCHECK defined in the Dockerfile, the Docker daemon and any higher-level orchestrator (like a local Kubernetes cluster or the Docker engine itself) cannot detect that the container is no longer performing its intended function. The attacker repeatedly triggers audits, spawning multiple 'zombie' containers that are never reaped or marked as unhealthy, eventually consuming all available host file descriptors, memory, and CPU cycles.\",\n    \"impact\": \"Denial of Service (DoS) of the Aegis Swarm platform. The host system becomes unresponsive, preventing legitimate security audits from being processed. In cloud environments, this could lead to increased operational costs before manual intervention occurs.\",\n    \"exploitCode\": \"1. Attacker prepares a repository with a script designed to hang: 'while true; do true; done'.\\n2. Attacker initiates multiple audit requests via the API:\\nfor i in {1..50}; do\\n  curl -X POST http:\\\\n    \"severity\": \"Low\",\\\\n    \"justification\": \"This is a reliability and availability issue. While it facilitates a DoS, it does not allow for privilege escalation, data exfiltration, or remote code execution. In a sandbox environment specifically designed to run untrusted code, availability is a secondary concern compared to isolation, though still relevant for system stability.\"\n  }\n]\n","timestamp":"2026-05-03 14:18:11"}
