{"level":"error","message":"JSON STRING THAT FAILED TO PARSE: \n[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"615c0667-646c-4243-bf6c-97c99ecbcb15\",\n    \"ruleId\": \"DS-0002\",\n    \"attackVector\": \"An attacker first gains Remote Code Execution (RCE) via the command injection vulnerability in `app.py` (the `run_command` function). Because the Dockerfile explicitly sets `USER root`, the attacker's payload executes with full administrative privileges within the container. They can then install network tools, modify system configurations, or access sensitive files like `/etc/shadow` to facilitate further movement.\",\n    \"impact\": \"Full container compromise. Root access allows the attacker to bypass file permissions, install persistence mechanisms (like a reverse shell in a cron job), and significantly increases the feasibility of a container escape to the host system.\",\n    \"exploitCode\": \"curl -X POST http:\n    \"severity\": \"High\",\n    \"justification\": \"While the tool rates this as a best practice, in the context of this application (which contains a direct command injection flaw in `app.py`), running as root is a critical escalatory factor. It turns a limited application-level exploit into a full system compromise.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"cc99f0f1-1061-4b62-b2f2-4993f8dfca0f\",\n    \"ruleId\": \"DS-0004\",\n    \"attackVector\": \"An attacker performs an external port scan (e.g., using Nmap) against the container's host. Upon discovering port 22 is exposed, they attempt to brute-force SSH credentials or use the `API_KEY` and `GITHUB_TOKEN` found in the environment variables as potential passwords, assuming credential reuse by the developers.\",\n    \"impact\": \"Unauthorized remote shell access. If successful, the attacker gains a persistent foothold that is often less monitored than web traffic.\",\n    \"exploitCode\": \"nmap -sV -p 22 <container_host_ip>\",\n    \"severity\": \"Medium\",\n    \"justification\": \"Exposing SSH is a violation of the principle of least privilege and increases the attack surface. However, it requires a secondary vulnerability (weak or leaked credentials) to be fully exploitable.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"2f5ad4b4-0225-4e6e-99ca-438248e56b70\",\n    \"ruleId\": \"DS-0026\",\n    \"attackVector\": \"An attacker triggers a resource exhaustion or a crash using the command injection in `app.py` (e.g., by executing a fork bomb). Since no `HEALTHCHECK` is defined, the container orchestration layer (like Kubernetes or Docker Swarm) continues to route traffic to the dead or unresponsive container rather than restarting it.\",\n    \"impact\": \"Sustained Denial of Service (DoS). The application becomes unavailable to legitimate users, and the system fails to self-heal.\",\n    \"exploitCode\": \"curl -X POST http:\n    \"severity\": \"Low\",\n    \"justification\": \"This is primarily an availability and reliability issue. It does not directly lead to data theft or unauthorized access, but it compounds the impact of other vulnerabilities.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"55111461-bead-4efc-96d9-96a00fe7e470\",\n    \"ruleId\": \"DS-0031\",\n    \"attackVector\": \"An attacker can discover this secret in two ways: 1) If they have access to the container image registry, they can run `docker inspect` on the image layers. 2) If they exploit the command injection in `app.py`, they can simply execute the `env` command to print all environment variables, including the `API_KEY`.\",\n    \"impact\": \"Credential theft. The attacker obtains the `API_KEY`, which can be used to access protected internal APIs or downstream services, leading to data breaches.\",\n    \"exploitCode\": \"curl -X POST http:\n    \"severity\": \"High\",\n    \"justification\": \"Hardcoding secrets in a Dockerfile is a critical security failure because environment variables are easily leaked through logs, inspection, and child processes. Given the existence of RCE in this app, this secret is effectively public.\"\n  }\n]\n","timestamp":"2026-05-03 14:20:15"}
